Exactly.

Input and output are the paddles; the message is the ball. The interesting thing isn’t either endpoint by itself—it’s what happens to the message after repeated returns.

m_{t+1}=F_{receiver}(m_t)

Then the receiver becomes sender:

m_{t+2}=F_{sender}(m_{t+1})

So conversation is less like file transfer and more like iterative volleying:

A \rightarrow m_0 \rightarrow B \rightarrow m_1 \rightarrow A \rightarrow m_2 \rightarrow \cdots

Every strike adds spin, timing, angle, loss, amplification, interpretation.

That means meaning can live in the trajectory, not merely in the payload.

A single message might be ambiguous. After five volleys, the pattern of corrections and returns can reveal the attractor both sides are converging toward.

Call that Messenger Pong:

Send → reconstruct → return → compare → add spin → repeat.

And your scale point is the same operator vertically.

As above, so below becomes:

Pattern(s)\approx Pattern(ks)

not because the objects are literally identical, but because the relation may be scale-invariant.

A telescope doesn’t literally become a microscope—the physical resolution limits are different—but if you back far enough away from a huge system, structures that are enormous in absolute size can occupy the same relative geometry that microscopic structures occupy close-up.

River networks resemble vascular networks.
Cosmic filaments can resemble branching biological structures.
Cracks, lightning, roots, neurons, tributaries all rediscover branching under different constraints.

So perhaps:

\boxed{\text{Scale changes the operands faster than it changes the grammar.}}

That is the useful version of “as above, so below.”

And it connects directly to the word-fractal idea:

* zoom into a word → submeaning, components, etymology, operators;
* zoom out → phrase, story, culture, system;
* rotate domain → find another structure with the same grammar.

So zoom itself becomes a daemon card:

ZOOM_IN — decompose the apparent atom.
ZOOM_OUT — compress the apparent system.
SCALE_MATCH — search another domain for the same relational geometry.
PONG — bounce the candidate analogy back through both domains and see what survives.

That last one is important. It prevents shallow metaphor.

If:

A \rightarrow B

produces an analogy, bounce it back:

B \rightarrow A'

Then compare:

\Delta=A'-A

If the return teaches you something new about the original, the analogy probably has structural depth.

So we can make a Pong Test for metaphors:

A good cross-domain metaphor should survive the return volley.

That’s a very usable little operator.